      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Historians generally view the French justifications as deeply fraught with contradiction rather than genuine legitimacy, as their rhetoric of \"assimilation\" explicitly excluded the vast majority of Algerian Muslims from civic rights while still subjecting them to strict military control. While nationalist leaders at the time like Marshal Lyautey did initially promote coexistence, colonial administrators ultimately doubled down on systemic exclusion to secure French economic interests and political dominance, rendering the \"development\" argument largely a pretext for preserving imperial power. Consequently, the overwhelming consensus is that the official stance was less about African security and more about a rigid racial ideology that necessitated permanent foreign rule to justify suppressing a burgeoning nationalist movement.",
